1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,960
After IVF, at the first ultrasound,
when do you see the heartbeat?

2
00:00:05,220 --> 00:00:08,060
Usually at around six
weeks of pregnancy.

3
00:00:08,220 --> 00:00:11,820
And if it’s too early,
that’s not necessarily a bad sign.

4
00:00:11,920 --> 00:00:13,180
I’ll explain everything.

5
00:00:13,340 --> 00:00:15,840
But first, a scene you all know.

6
00:00:16,080 --> 00:00:21,600
Admit it. You’re lying down,
and the gel is freezing — always freezing.

7
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:24,200
It’s article 4 of the Hippocratic Oath:

8
00:00:24,340 --> 00:00:27,460
ultrasound gel must
be stored in Siberia.

9
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:32,320
The probe is in place, the screen lights
up with a grainy black-and-white image,

10
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:36,600
like an eighties TV… and then:
the doctor goes quiet.

11
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:40,040
Three seconds that feel
like three centuries.

12
00:00:40,480 --> 00:00:43,440
Want to know what’s going on
in my head at that exact moment?

13
00:00:43,620 --> 00:00:45,700
Most of the time, nothing worrying.

14
00:00:45,860 --> 00:00:48,880
I’m just wrestling with the
little trackball on the machine,

15
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:53,340
trying to place a cursor to the
millimeter at the end of a tiny embryo.

16
00:00:53,380 --> 00:00:57,860
It’s about the only moment of the day
when a doctor stops talking voluntarily.

17
00:00:57,900 --> 00:01:01,900
But before that big silence,
there’s THE question.

18
00:01:02,060 --> 00:01:05,280
The one on your mind since the
blood test came back positive:

19
00:01:05,460 --> 00:01:09,160
the first ultrasound
after IVF — when is it?

20
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:11,060
And what should we really see?

21
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:15,020
Short answer: first,
a tiny bubble in the uterus.

22
00:01:15,180 --> 00:01:20,180
Then, usually at around six weeks of
pregnancy, a little cardiac activity.

23
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:24,780
And if the scan happens a bit early,
with no cardiac activity visible yet?

24
00:01:24,940 --> 00:01:27,260
That doesn’t necessarily
mean something’s wrong.

25
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:29,800
Sometimes it’s simply too early.

26
00:01:29,940 --> 00:01:33,140
Sometimes we need a follow-up
scan before we can conclude.

27
00:01:33,420 --> 00:01:36,800
I’m Dr Senai Aksoy,
fertility specialist.

28
00:01:36,920 --> 00:01:41,920
A quick note. In France, when people say
"the first ultrasound," they often mean

29
00:01:41,960 --> 00:01:43,420
the twelve-week scan.

30
00:01:43,480 --> 00:01:45,860
The one with the lovely profile photos.

31
00:01:46,040 --> 00:01:50,540
I’m talking about the VIP preview:
the one at your IVF center.

32
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:53,660
The very first one,
before the "first" one.

33
00:01:53,960 --> 00:01:56,420
Question one: the mystery of the weeks.

34
00:01:56,460 --> 00:02:02,460
A blastocyst — a five-day embryo —
was transferred exactly sixteen days ago.

35
00:02:02,620 --> 00:02:07,000
You count on your fingers: sixteen
days is barely more than two weeks.

36
00:02:07,160 --> 00:02:10,680
And then the doctor calmly
announces: "Congratulations,

37
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:12,640
you’re five weeks pregnant!"

38
00:02:13,180 --> 00:02:16,060
Excuse me? You count
again, with your toes.

39
00:02:16,180 --> 00:02:17,620
Who added three weeks?

40
00:02:17,720 --> 00:02:19,500
Has math changed since school?

41
00:02:19,980 --> 00:02:23,340
No, nobody cheated,
and math hasn’t changed.

42
00:02:23,480 --> 00:02:26,500
Welcome to obstetrics:
the only field in the world

43
00:02:26,540 --> 00:02:30,600
where your contract starts two weeks
before you’ve even met the employer!

44
00:02:30,760 --> 00:02:34,100
Two weeks of seniority,
straight away, on the house.

45
00:02:34,440 --> 00:02:38,120
We count the weeks of pregnancy from
the first day of your last period —

46
00:02:38,160 --> 00:02:40,740
that’s about two weeks
before fertilization.

47
00:02:40,780 --> 00:02:43,680
Even when it all started
in a dish in the lab,

48
00:02:43,740 --> 00:02:48,060
under the watchful eye of the
embryologist, we keep that calendar.

49
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:53,240
It’s the Greenwich Mean Time of pregnancy:
not necessarily your time zone,

50
00:02:53,280 --> 00:02:56,080
but every doctor sets their watch by it.

51
00:02:56,220 --> 00:03:01,560
With IVF, we know the exact date
to the day, but we still convert it.

52
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:04,620
On the day a five-day
blastocyst is transferred,

53
00:03:04,700 --> 00:03:07,740
you’re already two weeks
and five days pregnant.

54
00:03:07,820 --> 00:03:11,100
With a three-day embryo,
two weeks and three days.

55
00:03:11,220 --> 00:03:14,640
Two calendar weeks,
plus your blastocyst’s five days:

56
00:03:14,740 --> 00:03:18,040
that’s where your mysterious
almost-three weeks come from.

57
00:03:18,080 --> 00:03:22,820
And in France, to make things even
trickier, there’s also a second count,

58
00:03:22,880 --> 00:03:26,800
called "S-G" in French,
which starts at fertilization.

59
00:03:26,920 --> 00:03:28,560
So, two weeks less.

60
00:03:28,660 --> 00:03:31,480
Two different clocks
for a single uterus.

61
00:03:31,620 --> 00:03:35,560
To spare you the headache, I’m sticking
to the count from your last period.

62
00:03:35,920 --> 00:03:40,200
Key point: always ask your doctor
how many weeks pregnant you are,

63
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:43,340
counted from your last period,
and keep the transfer date

64
00:03:43,380 --> 00:03:45,560
and the embryo’s age somewhere safe.

65
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:49,360
Every ultrasound milestone
is counted that way.

66
00:03:49,400 --> 00:03:52,480
Question two:
what should we see, and when?

67
00:03:52,680 --> 00:03:56,180
At this stage, the scan
is usually done vaginally.

68
00:03:56,340 --> 00:03:58,760
The probe is right next to the uterus,

69
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:01,860
so it sees things much
earlier than through the belly.

70
00:04:02,020 --> 00:04:05,740
On screen, at first,
it’s a bit like looking at clouds:

71
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:08,440
everyone sees what they hope to see.

72
00:04:08,620 --> 00:04:12,760
The doctor, though, looks for
three very specific landmarks,

73
00:04:12,800 --> 00:04:14,400
always in the same order.

74
00:04:14,440 --> 00:04:16,640
One: the gestational sac.

75
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:20,300
A small, round,
dark pocket in the uterus.

76
00:04:20,460 --> 00:04:23,980
At around five weeks,
sometimes just a little earlier.

77
00:04:24,180 --> 00:04:25,820
Two: the yolk sac.

78
00:04:26,020 --> 00:04:27,740
Inside, a little ring.

79
00:04:27,820 --> 00:04:28,980
Sounds like breakfast.

80
00:04:29,180 --> 00:04:32,420
It isn’t. Usually a
bit after five weeks.

81
00:04:32,580 --> 00:04:36,900
When we see the sac with its yolk
sac inside the uterus, it’s official:

82
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:40,220
we’ve confirmed at least
one pregnancy in the uterus.

83
00:04:40,260 --> 00:04:43,920
But after IVF,
even though it’s extremely rare,

84
00:04:44,020 --> 00:04:47,600
we also check there isn’t a
second one in the wrong place.

85
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Three: the embryo. A few millimeters,
and inside it a little flicker:

86
00:04:52,540 --> 00:04:56,280
the cardiac activity,
usually at around six weeks.

87
00:04:56,480 --> 00:04:58,300
This table shows averages.

88
00:04:58,600 --> 00:05:02,840
The early studies were small,
but recent data confirm this timeline.

89
00:05:03,020 --> 00:05:07,800
Right at the start, being a few days
off is absolutely not enough to draw a

90
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:13,040
final conclusion. Meanwhile,
the doctor counts the sacs, one or two.

91
00:05:13,080 --> 00:05:17,600
And looks carefully beside the uterus,
toward the ovaries and the tubes.

92
00:05:18,320 --> 00:05:22,000
Question three: what if we
can’t see cardiac activity yet?

93
00:05:22,380 --> 00:05:24,500
Sometimes it’s simply too early.

94
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:27,180
Sometimes the pregnancy
has stopped developing.

95
00:05:27,220 --> 00:05:30,840
And a single early scan can’t
always tell the difference.

96
00:05:31,160 --> 00:05:34,100
That’s why the medical
criteria are very strict.

97
00:05:34,140 --> 00:05:38,880
Measurements to the millimeter:
the size of the sac and of the embryo.

98
00:05:38,920 --> 00:05:40,300
Not just impressions.

99
00:05:40,840 --> 00:05:43,460
The UK’s NICE guidelines are cautious.

100
00:05:43,660 --> 00:05:48,640
When a very early scan can’t give an answer,
a follow-up scan is often planned.

101
00:05:48,680 --> 00:05:50,620
At least seven days later.

102
00:05:50,740 --> 00:05:54,860
The decision depends mainly on the
size of the sac and of the embryo.

103
00:05:54,900 --> 00:05:59,300
And waiting for that scan has no negative
effect on how the pregnancy develops.

104
00:05:59,340 --> 00:06:02,640
Of course, if you have
worrying pain or bleeding,

105
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:04,720
you don’t just sit and wait a week.

106
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:10,900
A large American study, published online
in October 2025, adds something new.

107
00:06:11,080 --> 00:06:15,700
It looked at IVF pregnancies with
a sac clearly visible in the uterus.

108
00:06:15,880 --> 00:06:19,420
From six weeks and three days,
if there was no embryo,

109
00:06:19,460 --> 00:06:23,980
or an embryo without cardiac activity,
the pregnancy did not continue,

110
00:06:24,020 --> 00:06:25,980
in every case they observed.

111
00:06:26,120 --> 00:06:27,540
That’s an important result.

112
00:06:27,580 --> 00:06:30,840
But it’s a retrospective study,
from a single center,

113
00:06:30,880 --> 00:06:34,520
and these cut-offs aren’t
universal criteria yet.

114
00:06:34,860 --> 00:06:39,520
If your team suggests a follow-up scan,
it’s not to drag out the suspense.

115
00:06:39,640 --> 00:06:43,980
It’s to avoid a wrong diagnosis
and get a reliable answer.

116
00:06:44,220 --> 00:06:48,500
I know full well that this week
of waiting feels like an eternity.

117
00:06:48,940 --> 00:06:54,500
Key point: right at the start,
an incomplete picture isn’t enough to conclude.

118
00:06:54,580 --> 00:06:57,540
The follow-up scan is
there to clear up the doubt.

119
00:06:57,580 --> 00:07:00,300
And if the cardiac activity is visible?

120
00:07:00,520 --> 00:07:03,880
That’s excellent news, a real milestone.

121
00:07:04,180 --> 00:07:06,280
But let’s talk numbers, honestly.

122
00:07:06,480 --> 00:07:11,500
What’s your guess: out of ten IVF
pregnancies where we’ve seen the heartbeat,

123
00:07:11,640 --> 00:07:12,920
how many keep going?

124
00:07:13,180 --> 00:07:18,000
These are broad statistical benchmarks,
not your individual situation.

125
00:07:18,260 --> 00:07:21,120
An older American study,
from a single center,

126
00:07:21,160 --> 00:07:24,420
followed more than two
thousand IVF pregnancies

127
00:07:24,460 --> 00:07:28,020
with cardiac activity
seen at around seven weeks.

128
00:07:28,300 --> 00:07:32,180
Under 35, more than 9
in 10 kept developing.

129
00:07:32,320 --> 00:07:36,400
Between 35 and 39,
a little under 9 in 10.

130
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:40,020
And from 40 on,
a little under 8 in 10.

131
00:07:40,300 --> 00:07:44,480
A Chinese study published in
2016 found a similar picture.

132
00:07:44,660 --> 00:07:48,020
In more than two thousand
five hundred IVF pregnancies

133
00:07:48,060 --> 00:07:49,940
where the heartbeat had been seen,

134
00:07:50,120 --> 00:07:53,140
about 9 in 10 got
past the first trimester.

135
00:07:53,180 --> 00:07:56,420
And there too,
the risk went up with age.

136
00:07:56,880 --> 00:08:00,100
Some of these numbers are
a reminder to stay cautious.

137
00:08:00,260 --> 00:08:03,640
But they describe groups,
not your personal story.

138
00:08:03,840 --> 00:08:06,840
Seeing that heartbeat
is a major milestone.

139
00:08:06,960 --> 00:08:10,880
Not a promise carved in stone,
but a huge step forward.

140
00:08:11,500 --> 00:08:13,760
Now, safety. Straight talk.

141
00:08:14,600 --> 00:08:18,340
A pregnancy can sometimes
implant outside the uterus,

142
00:08:18,380 --> 00:08:22,540
most often in a fallopian tube:
that’s an ectopic pregnancy.

143
00:08:22,580 --> 00:08:28,340
In large IVF statistics, it affects
about 1 to 2 pregnancies in 100,

144
00:08:28,380 --> 00:08:32,840
and even fewer after transferring
a single frozen blastocyst.

145
00:08:32,960 --> 00:08:37,100
The risk is higher if your tubes
have been damaged or operated on,

146
00:08:37,140 --> 00:08:40,000
or if you’ve had an
ectopic pregnancy before.

147
00:08:40,180 --> 00:08:44,800
The early scan is there precisely to
check where the pregnancy implanted.

148
00:08:44,960 --> 00:08:49,340
And much more rarely, there can
be two pregnancies at the same time:

149
00:08:49,380 --> 00:08:51,960
one in the uterus, another outside it.

150
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,660
That’s called a heterotopic pregnancy.

151
00:08:54,800 --> 00:08:58,660
With IVF, it’s a few cases
per thousand, or fewer.

152
00:08:58,840 --> 00:09:02,540
That’s why the doctor also
checks both sides of the uterus.

153
00:09:02,960 --> 00:09:06,320
So, even after a reassuring
scan that morning,

154
00:09:06,520 --> 00:09:09,460
some signs should never wait
for the next appointment.

155
00:09:09,720 --> 00:09:13,460
Sharp pain in the lower belly,
especially on one side.

156
00:09:13,640 --> 00:09:15,860
Unusual pain at the tip of the shoulder.

157
00:09:15,900 --> 00:09:18,460
Feeling faint, or passing out.

158
00:09:18,700 --> 00:09:20,120
Or heavy bleeding.

159
00:09:20,380 --> 00:09:23,320
In those cases,
you don’t ask the internet.

160
00:09:23,380 --> 00:09:28,060
You go straight to the emergency
department, or call 15 in France,

161
00:09:28,200 --> 00:09:32,240
one-one-two anywhere in Europe,
or your local emergency number.

162
00:09:32,580 --> 00:09:36,280
For light bleeding or brown
discharge, contact your center.

163
00:09:36,380 --> 00:09:40,980
For persistent pain, even moderate,
call your team the same day as well.

164
00:09:41,200 --> 00:09:44,220
And your support medications,
progesterone included:

165
00:09:44,260 --> 00:09:47,100
keep taking them exactly as prescribed,

166
00:09:47,140 --> 00:09:49,940
until the exact date
your center gave you.

167
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:54,340
Even after a beautiful image,
you never stop a treatment on your own.

168
00:09:54,620 --> 00:09:56,240
The data end here.

169
00:09:56,440 --> 00:10:01,760
My clinical opinion starts now:
we all want to know everything, right away.

170
00:10:01,900 --> 00:10:04,760
Me included. When I’m
waiting for a parcel,

171
00:10:04,820 --> 00:10:09,460
I refresh the tracking every four
minutes: "Parcel at sorting center…

172
00:10:09,660 --> 00:10:10,600
driver on the way…"

173
00:10:10,640 --> 00:10:14,540
Does refreshing the screen make
the driver arrive any faster?

174
00:10:15,060 --> 00:10:20,600
Absolutely not. A scan at around five
weeks can sometimes help us locate the

175
00:10:20,640 --> 00:10:25,020
pregnancy. But if the question is
"Is the embryo developing well?",

176
00:10:25,120 --> 00:10:28,140
it’s likely to leave us
with an incomplete answer.

177
00:10:28,240 --> 00:10:30,680
Unless there’s a
specific medical reason,

178
00:10:30,720 --> 00:10:33,960
I’d rather have one
well-timed scan at around six

179
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:37,500
or seven weeks than three
appointments booked too early.

180
00:10:37,720 --> 00:10:40,040
So, to answer the
question from the start:

181
00:10:40,080 --> 00:10:43,640
we usually see the heartbeat
at around six weeks.

182
00:10:43,700 --> 00:10:47,940
That’s why the first scan is often
scheduled between six and seven weeks,

183
00:10:47,980 --> 00:10:49,340
depending on the center.

184
00:10:49,420 --> 00:10:51,700
And if it’s too early to see everything,

185
00:10:51,860 --> 00:10:55,620
a follow-up at least a week
later helps make things clearer.

186
00:10:55,840 --> 00:10:59,940
Next time your doctor goes into that
big silence in front of the screen,

187
00:11:00,300 --> 00:11:02,360
breathe calmly. Most of the time,

188
00:11:02,400 --> 00:11:05,060
they’re just chasing a
millimeter with the cursor.

189
00:11:05,120 --> 00:11:09,560
And if the silence drags on,
you have every right to ask: "Doctor,

190
00:11:09,620 --> 00:11:10,760
what are you measuring?"

191
00:11:10,960 --> 00:11:14,100
For your appointment,
three questions to write down.

192
00:11:14,680 --> 00:11:17,880
Exactly how many weeks pregnant
will I be on the day of the scan,

193
00:11:17,920 --> 00:11:19,960
and what do you expect to see?

194
00:11:20,100 --> 00:11:23,640
If not everything is visible yet,
when do we do the follow-up,

195
00:11:23,680 --> 00:11:25,600
and who will give me the results?

196
00:11:25,760 --> 00:11:29,400
And until what exact date should
I keep taking my medications,

197
00:11:29,440 --> 00:11:31,780
and who decides when to stop?

198
00:11:31,880 --> 00:11:35,260
These questions are listed just
below, in the video description.

199
00:11:35,300 --> 00:11:38,060
This content is for information only

200
00:11:38,100 --> 00:11:41,120
and doesn’t replace a
personal medical assessment.
